<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Eichstätt</h1><p class="st-meta">1305 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Eichstätt</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Franconian church principality on the Altmühl, founded on the see of Saint Willibald, that became a showcase of Catholic Baroque.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a> made his kinsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willibald">Willibald</a> the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichstatt">Eichstätt</a> in the 740s. For centuries the bishops held little secular land, but in 1305 they inherited much of the lordship of the extinct counts of Hirschberg, laying the foundation of a territorial principality along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altmuhl">Altmühl</a> valley between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, elected by the cathedral chapter, governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichstatt">Eichstätt</a> and the hilltop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willibaldsburg">Willibaldsburg</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-conrad-von-gemmingen">Johann Conrad von Gemmingen</a> laid out the garden recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hortus-eystettensis">Hortus Eystettensis</a> of 1613. The territory belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconian-circle">Franconian Circle</a> and lived from farming, forests and limestone quarrying. It remained Catholic, and after the Swedish sack of 1634 the city was rebuilt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style by architects such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriel-de-gabrieli">Gabriel de Gabrieli</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1802 the prince-bishopric was secularised. Its lands went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, then largely to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-grand-duke-of-tuscany">Ferdinand of Tuscany</a>'s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-salzburg">Electorate of Salzburg</a>, before passing to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria</a> in 1805. The diocese survives, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichstatt">Eichstätt</a> is home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany's</a> only Catholic university.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-eichstatt.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Eichstätt" width="320" height="273"><figcaption>Image: Johann Homann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince-Bishopric_of_Eichst%C3%A4tt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1305 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Eichstätt</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gabriel von Eyb, Johann Conrad von Gemmingen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Eichstätt</dd></dl></aside></div>
